; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Euler Spiral and Smooth Roads
; CATEGORY: Spirals & Curves
; LEVEL:    Advanced
; TAGS:     clothoid, curvature, euler spiral, engineering, graphs
; SUMMARY:  Draws the Euler spiral, whose bend grows steadily with distance, and shows why road and railway builders use it.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   At the top, the turtle walks equal steps and turns a tiny bit
;   more at every step: 0, then 0.02, then 0.04 degrees, and so on.
;   The bend (the "curvature") grows steadily, so the path winds
;   into a tighter and tighter curl. Doing the same backwards from
;   the start gives the second curl. This is the Euler spiral.
;
;   At the bottom are two roads that both turn 90 degrees. The left
;   one goes straight into a circle, so the steering wheel must jump
;   suddenly. The right one eases in and out along pieces of Euler
;   spiral, so the steering changes smoothly. Under each road a graph
;   shows how hard the road bends at each point.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Change GROWTH for a looser or tighter spiral.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * curvature: how many degrees the path turns per step
;   * the Euler spiral (or clothoid): curvature grows in a straight line
;   * recording values as you draw, then plotting them as a graph
;   * real engineering: railways and highway ramps use this curve
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Make the turns grow faster (0.05) or slower (0.01).
;   * Make the easing pieces in SMOOTHROAD longer. When does the
;     middle circle disappear completely?
;   * Grow the turn by the SQUARE of the step number. What curve now?
; ============================================================

make "growth 0.02
make "turns []

; One half of the spiral: :steps steps, the turn growing every step.
; :side is 1 for right turns and -1 for left turns.
to curl :steps :side
  for [i 0 :steps] [
    forward 2
    right :side * :growth * :i
  ]
end

; ---- roads ---------------------------------------------------------------
; Every step of a road is 2 long. We remember each step's turn in the
; list TURNS so the graph can be drawn afterwards.

to roadstep :turn
  forward 2
  left :turn
  make "turns lput :turn :turns
end

; Straight, then a quarter circle (1.4 degrees every step), then straight.
to suddenroad
  repeat 50 [roadstep 0]
  repeat 64 [roadstep 90 / 64]
  repeat 50 [roadstep 0]
end

; Straight, then the turn grows steadily from 0 to 1.4 (an Euler
; spiral), a short circle, the turn shrinks steadily back to 0, straight.
; Each easing piece turns 1.4 x (1 + 2 + ... + 30) / 30 = 21.7 degrees,
; and the circle in the middle makes up the rest of the 90.
to smoothroad
  repeat 50 [roadstep 0]
  for [i 1 30] [roadstep 1.4 * :i / 30]
  repeat int (90 - 2 * 21.7) / 1.4 [roadstep 1.4]
  for [i 30 1] [roadstep 1.4 * :i / 30]
  repeat 50 [roadstep 0]
end

; Draw a road, wide and grey, starting at (x, y) heading right.
; RUN calls the road procedure whose name we were given.
to drawroad :x :y :road
  penup setxy :x :y setheading 90
  setpencolor [90 90 100] setpensize 16 pendown
  make "turns []
  run (list :road)
end

; A bar graph of the recorded turns, left edge at (x, y).
to graph :x :y :label
  setpencolor [180 180 180] setpensize 1
  penup setxy :x :y pendown setxy :x + (count :turns) :y
  setpencolor [210 80 60] setpensize 2
  penup setxy :x :y
  foreach :turns [
    pendown setxy :x + # :y + 30 * ?
  ]
  penup setxy :x :y - 20 setpencolor [80 80 80]
  label :label
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [240 244 236]
; the Euler spiral, both halves from the same starting point
setpencolor [50 90 180] setpensize 2
penup setxy 0 95 setheading 90 pendown
curl 520 1
penup setxy 0 95 setheading 270 pendown
curl 520 1
setlabelheight 14
drawroad -360 -200 "suddenroad
graph -360 -280 [sudden: the bend jumps]
drawroad 60 -200 "smoothroad
graph 60 -280 [smooth: the bend grows and shrinks]
